# freshclam.conf for the DocFlow pilot machine (C5.3).
#
# Copy to C:\Program Files\ClamAV\freshclam.conf, then run ONCE by hand — the
# first download is ~250 MB and needs internet:
#   "C:\Program Files\ClamAV\freshclam.exe"
# then install it as a service so it keeps itself current:
#   "C:\Program Files\ClamAV\freshclam.exe" --install
#   Start-Service freshclam
#
# A scanner with month-old signatures is a scanner that will pass the thing it
# was installed to catch. `/settings/system` shows the signature age, and
# verify.ps1 warns past seven days — but this file is what keeps it fresh.

# As with clamd.conf: the stock file ships with `Example` set and will not run
# until it is removed.
# Example

DatabaseDirectory C:\ProgramData\ClamAV\db
DatabaseMirror database.clamav.net
UpdateLogFile C:\ProgramData\ClamAV\freshclam.log
LogFileMaxSize 10M
LogRotate yes
LogTime yes
PidFile C:\ProgramData\ClamAV\freshclam.pid

# Twelve times a day is ClamAV's own recommendation and is polite to the
# mirrors. The firm PC is on all day; there is no reason to check less often.
Checks 12

# Tell clamd to reload as soon as new signatures land, so an update does not
# wait for the next restart.
NotifyClamd C:\Program Files\ClamAV\clamd.conf

# A firm PC behind a corporate proxy needs these; most do not.
# HTTPProxyServer proxy.firm.local
# HTTPProxyPort 8080

# If the network blocks database.clamav.net, freshclam fails silently in its own
# log. That is what the seven-day warning in verify.ps1 and the system status
# panel are for — someone finds out in a week, not in an incident.
ConnectTimeout 60
ReceiveTimeout 120
